{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787149016,"nonce":"mv4cKQ+HpWuWAE7av4zF9ap67k8=","title":"Twenty places still describe the deleted unacked reminder and its mention audience as live, including a CI lint that tells a developer to say `unacked_reminder` and two lines of CONTEXT.md that contradict each other","message":"**What is wrong.** `4f5204f` deleted the unacked reminder and the whole Slack mention subsystem root and branch, across 88 files, and it did the hard part well: roughly 55 sites carry the house `⛔ … WAS HERE AND IS DELETED (git-bug bd0fb1d)` marker correctly. Twenty do not. They describe the reminder or the mention mechanism in the PRESENT TENSE, or as a live design constraint, and a reader acts on them. Three of the twenty are worse than stale prose: one is an active CI message, one is a self-contradiction inside a single file, and three are the OpenAPI contract, which is operator-facing.\n\n**The demonstration.** The two sharpest, both verified by reading the lines rather than grepping them:\n\n`tools/lintvocab/main.go:100` is the violation message for the still-active banned word `escalation`:\n\n```go\n{\"escalation\", regexp.MustCompile(`(?i)escalat(e|es|ed|ing|ion|ions|ory)`),\n    \"oto has ONE reminder stage that ends at a CHANNEL; an escalation is a ladder that ends at a PERSON (SPEC §G.9.1). Say unacked_reminder.\"},\n```\n\noto has ZERO reminder stages. A developer who trips this gate today — and `lint-vocabulary` reports 60 violations, so it trips — is told to rename to a term that exists nowhere in the tree. The rule itself is correct and must stay: `CONTEXT.md:59` says so in as many words, *\"`escalation` remains a banned word\"*. Only the remedy is wrong.\n\nAnd `CONTEXT.md` contradicts itself 48 lines apart. `:59` reads *\"**No unprompted reminder, at all.** The one reminder stage was withdrawn (git-bug `bd0fb1d`) … This SUPERSEDES \"one stage, forever\" and is strictly stronger\"*. `:107` reads *\"Say **firing duration**, not MTTR. Say **unacked reminder**, not escalation.\"* The vocabulary section recommends the term the scope section has withdrawn.\n\n**Why it matters.** This repo's culture is refusing to ship claims it cannot back, and it has filed tickets about exactly this defect class — `e5c060b` was a comment that lied about a state nothing could enter, and closing it was worth a migration. A stale comment is not cosmetic here for three concrete reasons. `internal/channels/render/slack/renderer.go:36-38` asserts that a mention audience *\"now arrives per delivery, already resolved and already gated on severity, as `RenderOptions.Mentions`\"* — and it sits ONE PARAGRAPH BELOW the correctly-marked `⛔` at `:27` in the same comment block, so the block both denies and asserts the field. `internal/notification/domain/mode.go:280-284` describes `PlanFor`'s order of operations as three numbered steps beginning *\"1. the reminder is decided first\"*, while the function body a few lines down carries its own correct `⛔` note saying that step is deleted: the overview and the implementation disagree inside one function. And `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:9018` still documents the `thread_updates` exception — *\"except the unacked reminder, which is always broadcast\"* — that `docs/design/SPEC.md:4116` already records as removed, so the SPEC and the contract now disagree with each other in the reader's favour nowhere.\n\n**Where.** Twenty sites, grouped by what fixing each one costs.\n\nProse-only, inside the notification and channels modules: `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go:67-76` (describes a mention added to the top-level `text`; the two lines under it are `sentence := replyText(v)` then `text := sentence`) · `internal/channels/render/slack/renderer.go:36-38` · `internal/channels/providers/slack/channel.go:88-90` (*\"Broadcast is used sparingly and only for the unacked reminder … at most once per generation (§G.9)\"*; broadcast is now driven by `BroadcastPolicy.Warrants`) · `internal/channels/domain/ports.go:148` (*\"oto has one reminder stage and it reminds a CHANNEL\"*, inside the `ModeBroadcastReply` doc) · `internal/notification/domain/mode.go:280-284` · `internal/notification/api/mapper.go:13-16` and `:51` (a `seconds ⇄ Duration` conversion for a field the function no longer has, plus a dangling *\"as it does for the reminder above\"*).\n\nProse-only, elsewhere in the tree: `internal/app/adapters.go:737-741` (claims `NotificationDefaults` returns a reminder-delay fallback; the body returns `{Broadcast, Verbosity}`) · `internal/identity/repository/orgs.go:54-56` (a paragraph describing four JSON keys, with no struct fields after it — *\"these\"* has no antecedent) · `tools/lintvocab/main.go:100` · `CONTEXT.md:107` · `deploy/slack/manifest.yaml:76-77` (the `chat:write` scope justified by *\"the broadcast reply used by the unacked reminder\"*) · `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:177-179` · `docs/adr/0013-alert-first-scope-boundary.md:122-167` (*\"`unacked_reminder_after_s` is still a scalar\"*, with no superseded marker — unlike ADR 0020, which has one) · `docs/design/notification-content-customisation.md:207-208,302-315` · `docs/runbooks/oto_ingest_process_duration_seconds.md:19` · `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:218` and `:605` (`:605` is END-USER Settings copy naming the reminder as a fixed broadcast).\n\nContract, and therefore also the generated client: `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:4869` (`getOrgSettings` lists a deleted *\"unacked-reminder default\"* knob) · `:9018-9019` · `:10083-10085` (*\"The other two — `refired` and `unacked_reminder` — are fixed by policy\"*). All three are DESCRIPTION text only, so nothing about the contract's shape changes and no breaking-change declaration is owed — but `web/src/api/generated/schema.d.ts:2122,4727,5438` mirror them and gate G3 compares the client byte for byte, so the client must be regenerated rather than hand-edited.\n\nDead code found alongside: `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go:76` `text := sentence`, an alias whose only purpose was the mention it no longer has · `internal/notification/service/digest_tick_test.go:93-97` and `notify_seq_test.go:29-32`, two mock `ListWithUnackedReminder` stubs.\n\n**Scope.** medium. Twenty sites across roughly fifteen files, no behaviour change and no migration. Two of the twenty need a decision rather than a deletion, which is why they are called out below: `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:10083` and `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:605` both describe *\"the other two\"* fixed broadcasts, and the accurate current set is awkward to state — `refired` has no producer at all (`internal/notification/domain/broadcast.go:71`, *\"⛔ RETAINED FOR HISTORY; NOTHING PRODUCES IT\"*), so the honest sentence is that `broadcast_on_resolved` is the only broadcast anything can currently reach. Whether operator-facing copy should say that, or should describe the shape rather than today's emptiness, is a product call.\n\n⚠️ `docs-site/**` is deliberately EXCLUDED and is not one of the twenty. It is generated from `docs/` by `docs-site/scripts/sync-docs.mjs` and its checked-in copy was last synced the day before the deletion, so nearly all of it still describes the reminder as live. That is sync lag in a generated mirror, not hand-authored stale prose, and house practice is to edit `docs/` and let the generator catch up.\n\n**Done when.** Every one of the twenty sites either becomes true or carries the house `⛔ … git-bug bd0fb1d` marker in the past tense, `web/src/api/generated/**` is REGENERATED rather than edited, and a repeat sweep for present-tense reminder or mention prose returns nothing a reader would act on. The two sites needing a product call are either answered or split out into their own ticket rather than left silently in this one.","files":null}]}